From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: A7om <hatter6822@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/intc/arm_gic: Honour GICD_SGIR.NSATT with the Security
 Extensions

With the Security Extensions, a write to GICD_SGIR forwards an SGI to a
target only if the SGI is configured there as the group NSATT names:
Group 0 for NSATT 0, Group 1 for NSATT 1. Non-secure writes act as if
NSATT were 1, whatever they write, so the Non-secure world can never
raise a Secure (Group 0) SGI (GIC architecture specification v2,
section 4.3.15).

The model ignored NSATT and the security of the access, so Non-secure
software could make any SGI pending on any core, including the Group 0
SGIs a Secure monitor relies on, and a Secure write meant for Group 1
could hit a core that had the SGI as Group 0.

Drop the targets whose configured group does not match before marking
the SGI pending. Without the Security Extensions nothing changes.
---
 hw/intc/arm_gic.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/intc/arm_gic.c b/hw/intc/arm_gic.c
index 4d4b79e..c94809a 100644
--- a/hw/intc/arm_gic.c
+++ b/hw/intc/arm_gic.c
@@ -1522,6 +1522,20 @@ static void gic_dist_writel(void *opaque, hwaddr offset,
             mask = ALL_CPU_MASK;
             break;
         }
+        if (s->security_extn) {
+            /*
+             * NSATT selects the group an SGI must be configured as on its
+             * target to be forwarded there; a Non-secure write only ever
+             * raises Group 1 SGIs, whatever its NSATT bit.
+             */
+            bool group1 = !attrs.secure || (value & (1 << 15));
+
+            for (target_cpu = 0; target_cpu < GIC_NCPU; target_cpu++) {
+                if (GIC_DIST_TEST_GROUP(irq, 1 << target_cpu) != group1) {
+                    mask &= ~(1 << target_cpu);
+                }
+            }
+        }
         GIC_DIST_SET_PENDING(irq, mask);
         target_cpu = ctz32(mask);
         while (target_cpu < GIC_NCPU) {
